1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,320
Hello and welcome to Cozy French Books.

2
00:00:03,320 --> 00:00:04,760
I'm Léa.

3
00:00:04,760 --> 00:00:08,420
Here, we open beautiful books and take

4
00:00:08,420 --> 00:00:09,960
the time to see what they can

5
00:00:09,960 --> 00:00:12,520
teach us about our own lives.

6
00:00:12,520 --> 00:00:14,570
Today, I'd like to tell you

7
00:00:14,570 --> 00:00:16,600
about The Alchemist, by Paulo Coelho.

8
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:18,700
And there's a question I'd like

9
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:21,240
to follow with you all through this episode.

10
00:00:21,240 --> 00:00:24,080
Where does real motivation come from?

11
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:26,320
When you're twenty, you sometimes wonder

12
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:28,890
what you want to do with your life.

13
00:00:28,940 --> 00:00:31,640
A few years later, the question changes.

14
00:00:31,640 --> 00:00:33,690
I've talked about it for so long… what

15
00:00:33,690 --> 00:00:35,800
have I really done to get closer to it?

16
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:37,990
And then one day you realise

17
00:00:37,990 --> 00:00:40,520
that this dream is still there.

18
00:00:40,520 --> 00:00:42,800
It has travelled through the years with us.

19
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:44,510
So you wonder if it's still

20
00:00:44,510 --> 00:00:46,520
worth starting.

21
00:00:46,520 --> 00:00:50,040
If you've ever had this thought, stay with me.

22
00:00:50,040 --> 00:00:51,930
You might recognise a part

23
00:00:51,930 --> 00:00:54,250
of yourself in the story this book tells.

24
00:00:54,300 --> 00:00:56,440
The starting point is simple.

25
00:00:56,440 --> 00:00:59,360
A young Spanish shepherd, Santiago,

26
00:00:59,360 --> 00:01:01,920
dreams of a treasure near the pyramids of Egypt.

27
00:01:01,920 --> 00:01:04,440
To go and find it, he has to leave the life

28
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:06,760
he knows and part with his sheep.

29
00:01:06,760 --> 00:01:09,920
It all sounds like an adventure tale.

30
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:11,880
But what made me want to tell you about it

31
00:01:11,880 --> 00:01:14,370
is the way Coelho shows the moments

32
00:01:14,370 --> 00:01:17,160
when Santiago could easily do something else.

33
00:01:17,160 --> 00:01:19,000
He could keep what he has,

34
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:21,920
go home after a bad experience, or stay

35
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:24,360
somewhere he's finally happy.

36
00:01:24,360 --> 00:01:26,320
Each of these choices makes sense.

37
00:01:26,320 --> 00:01:28,180
That's why we can recognise ourselves

38
00:01:28,180 --> 00:01:30,240
in this boy, even if we've never

39
00:01:30,240 --> 00:01:32,280
wanted to look for gold in the desert.

40
00:01:32,280 --> 00:01:35,060
Before The Alchemist, Coelho had already written

41
00:01:35,060 --> 00:01:38,360
about his pilgrimage to Santiago de Compostela.

42
00:01:38,360 --> 00:01:40,850
A pilgrimage is a journey you make

43
00:01:40,850 --> 00:01:43,560
for a religious or spiritual reason.

44
00:01:43,560 --> 00:01:46,050
In this novel we find the idea of a

45
00:01:46,050 --> 00:01:49,050
journey that changes the person who makes it.

46
00:01:49,100 --> 00:01:50,540
A road can be long.

47
00:01:50,540 --> 00:01:53,200
But when you know what you're looking for,

48
00:01:53,200 --> 00:01:55,800
you can understand why you take the next step.

49
00:01:55,800 --> 00:01:57,510
And that's where Santiago's first choice

50
00:01:57,510 --> 00:01:59,640
becomes interesting.

51
00:01:59,640 --> 00:02:02,450
When you keep counting what you could lose,

52
00:02:02,450 --> 00:02:03,990
you can end up forgetting

53
00:02:03,990 --> 00:02:05,640
what you wanted in the first place.

54
00:02:05,640 --> 00:02:07,350
Look at the choice Santiago's family

55
00:02:07,350 --> 00:02:09,320
had made for him.

56
00:02:09,320 --> 00:02:11,840
His parents wanted him to become a priest.

57
00:02:11,840 --> 00:02:14,240
For them, it was a stable life.

58
00:02:14,240 --> 00:02:15,740
They had put a lot of

59
00:02:15,740 --> 00:02:17,840
hope into it, and Santiago knew it.

60
00:02:17,890 --> 00:02:21,280
He, for a long time, had wanted to travel.

61
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:23,910
So at some point, he had to look at his father

62
00:02:23,910 --> 00:02:26,960
and tell him he wanted a different life.

63
00:02:26,960 --> 00:02:29,270
You can feel how hard that

64
00:02:29,270 --> 00:02:32,240
conversation is without knowing the rest.

65
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:34,560
How do you say what you want to someone who

66
00:02:34,560 --> 00:02:37,720
spent years wanting the best for you?

67
00:02:37,720 --> 00:02:39,620
His father ends up giving him three gold

68
00:02:39,620 --> 00:02:41,800
coins so he can buy some sheep.

69
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:44,080
It's a small gesture in the novel,

70
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:46,380
and I like what it says about the father.

71
00:02:46,430 --> 00:02:48,920
He too had wanted to see the world.

72
00:02:48,920 --> 00:02:51,000
By giving these coins to his son,

73
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:53,920
he gives him the chance to try a life

74
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:56,120
he had once imagined for himself.

75
00:02:56,120 --> 00:02:57,380
The book comes back to this

76
00:02:57,380 --> 00:02:59,200
idea with other people.

77
00:02:59,200 --> 00:03:02,000
The popcorn seller wants to travel.

78
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:05,480
The crystal merchant thinks about Mecca.

79
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:08,110
Even near the pyramids, we'll hear a man

80
00:03:08,110 --> 00:03:10,960
talk about a treasure he saw in a dream.

81
00:03:10,960 --> 00:03:13,330
Dreams are everywhere in this story.

82
00:03:13,380 --> 00:03:15,800
What changes is the place each

83
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:18,320
person gives them in their day.

84
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:20,050
Santiago becomes a shepherd

85
00:03:20,050 --> 00:03:22,400
and goes from village to village.

86
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:24,450
He even comes to like this

87
00:03:24,450 --> 00:03:26,400
life enough to hesitate to leave it.

88
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:28,470
That matters, because you might

89
00:03:28,470 --> 00:03:30,560
think that someone who has dared once

90
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:33,160
will always know how to do it again.

91
00:03:33,160 --> 00:03:34,950
But the first time,

92
00:03:34,950 --> 00:03:37,840
he wanted a life he had never had.

93
00:03:37,840 --> 00:03:39,730
This time, he risks losing

94
00:03:39,730 --> 00:03:42,170
a life he built himself.

95
00:03:42,220 --> 00:03:44,910
His sheep mean work,

96
00:03:44,910 --> 00:03:47,720
money and a kind of freedom.

97
00:03:47,720 --> 00:03:48,990
The treasure, though,

98
00:03:48,990 --> 00:03:52,280
only exists in a dream that keeps coming back.

99
00:03:52,280 --> 00:03:53,880
We understand that he needs

100
00:03:53,880 --> 00:03:55,360
time to decide.

101
00:03:55,360 --> 00:03:56,640
The King of Salem,

102
00:03:56,640 --> 00:03:59,940
an old man Santiago meets,

103
00:03:59,940 --> 00:04:02,280
invites him to take this dream seriously.

104
00:04:02,280 --> 00:04:04,600
He talks to him about his Personal Legend.

105
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:07,090
In the book's language, it's the path

106
00:04:07,090 --> 00:04:10,410
you feel you truly want to follow.

107
00:04:10,460 --> 00:04:12,910
Santiago can then go back to a question

108
00:04:12,910 --> 00:04:15,440
that existed before the pyramids.

109
00:04:15,440 --> 00:04:17,720
Why did he become a shepherd?

110
00:04:17,720 --> 00:04:20,510
To earn a living, of course,

111
00:04:20,510 --> 00:04:22,680
and also to see the world.

112
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:24,530
The search for the treasure gives him

113
00:04:24,530 --> 00:04:26,670
a new way to do what has mattered

114
00:04:26,670 --> 00:04:28,440
to him for a long time.

115
00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:30,510
When something tient à cœur, it means

116
00:04:30,510 --> 00:04:33,040
it matters a lot to you,

117
00:04:33,040 --> 00:04:35,480
even when it's hard to explain why.

118
00:04:35,480 --> 00:04:39,530
And you, when you look at a big decision,

119
00:04:39,580 --> 00:04:43,040
what comes to mind first?

120
00:04:43,040 --> 00:04:45,870
The things you know.

121
00:04:45,870 --> 00:04:48,280
What you could lose already has a shape.

122
00:04:48,280 --> 00:04:49,650
You know how long

123
00:04:49,650 --> 00:04:51,480
it took you to earn your money.

124
00:04:51,480 --> 00:04:52,980
You know what a change

125
00:04:52,980 --> 00:04:54,640
would ask of your family.

126
00:04:54,640 --> 00:04:57,650
But what you could learn

127
00:04:57,650 --> 00:05:01,040
or discover by trying still feels far away.

128
00:05:01,040 --> 00:05:02,820
It's easy to spend all

129
00:05:02,820 --> 00:05:05,640
your evenings counting the same risks again.

130
00:05:05,640 --> 00:05:06,160
In the end,

131
00:05:06,160 --> 00:05:08,770
you know very well what you could lose.

132
00:05:08,820 --> 00:05:11,210
Sometimes we forget to ask ourselves

133
00:05:11,210 --> 00:05:14,000
what we could still become.

134
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:18,160
In the book, Santiago finally takes the plunge.

135
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:21,160
He parts with his flock and takes the road.

136
00:05:21,160 --> 00:05:23,650
Se lancer means deciding to take the first

137
00:05:23,650 --> 00:05:25,640
step into something new.

138
00:05:25,640 --> 00:05:28,760
That first step is worth pausing on.

139
00:05:28,760 --> 00:05:30,810
You can be very afraid of regretting

140
00:05:30,810 --> 00:05:32,680
a decision made too quickly.

141
00:05:32,680 --> 00:05:35,180
You can also wonder if, in a few

142
00:05:35,180 --> 00:05:38,880
years, you'll regret never having tried.

143
00:05:38,880 --> 00:05:41,160
Both questions deserve to be asked.

144
00:05:41,160 --> 00:05:43,010
Many people give themselves

145
00:05:43,010 --> 00:05:45,760
years to answer the first one,

146
00:05:45,760 --> 00:05:48,000
then find the second one a bit late,

147
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:51,000
in a passing conversation, or finding

148
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:53,040
a project's name on an old sheet of paper.

149
00:05:53,040 --> 00:05:56,040
When a wish you had put aside comes back,

150
00:05:56,040 --> 00:05:58,200
use the moment to do

151
00:05:58,200 --> 00:05:59,320
something concrete.

152
00:05:59,320 --> 00:06:01,810
Maybe all you need is to look up

153
00:06:01,810 --> 00:06:04,160
some information, call someone

154
00:06:04,160 --> 00:06:06,330
or find out when you could give it a try.

155
00:06:06,380 --> 00:06:08,610
One small action is enough to get out

156
00:06:08,610 --> 00:06:11,440
of a question you always answer

157
00:06:11,440 --> 00:06:12,640
the same way.

158
00:06:12,640 --> 00:06:14,690
It can also show you that your idea

159
00:06:14,690 --> 00:06:16,960
doesn't appeal to you as much as you thought.

160
00:06:16,960 --> 00:06:19,320
You'll have learned that by getting closer.

161
00:06:19,320 --> 00:06:21,370
If you want to keep going,

162
00:06:21,370 --> 00:06:23,400
you'll already know which way to go.

163
00:06:23,400 --> 00:06:25,450
The book's second idea appears

164
00:06:25,450 --> 00:06:27,420
when everything seems to prove right those

165
00:06:27,420 --> 00:06:29,640
who told you to stay put.

166
00:06:29,640 --> 00:06:31,280
You had the courage to leave,

167
00:06:31,280 --> 00:06:33,360
and something happens that makes you

168
00:06:33,360 --> 00:06:35,830
think you made a mistake.

169
00:06:35,880 --> 00:06:38,000
That's what happens in Tangier.

170
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,490
Santiago doesn't speak Arabic yet, and he's

171
00:06:40,490 --> 00:06:43,760
relieved to meet a man who speaks Spanish.

172
00:06:43,760 --> 00:06:45,280
He trusts him.

173
00:06:45,280 --> 00:06:47,480
The man disappears with his money.

174
00:06:47,480 --> 00:06:48,800
Shortly before,

175
00:06:48,800 --> 00:06:51,720
Santiago had sheep and a trade.

176
00:06:51,720 --> 00:06:54,410
Now he doesn't know how he'll

177
00:06:54,410 --> 00:06:57,240
eat, or how he could get home.

178
00:06:57,240 --> 00:06:58,810
In a moment like that,

179
00:06:58,810 --> 00:07:01,630
the first thing that disappears, sometimes,

180
00:07:01,630 --> 00:07:04,010
is the image you had of yourself.

181
00:07:04,060 --> 00:07:05,680
Santiago saw himself as someone

182
00:07:05,680 --> 00:07:07,880
who had made a brave decision.

183
00:07:07,880 --> 00:07:11,040
After the theft, he could see himself as someone

184
00:07:11,040 --> 00:07:13,920
who was wrong to trust a stranger.

185
00:07:13,920 --> 00:07:15,560
We know this way of jumping

186
00:07:15,560 --> 00:07:17,480
very fast from one thing to another.

187
00:07:17,480 --> 00:07:19,720
A project goes badly, and within a few

188
00:07:19,720 --> 00:07:22,720
hours we turn it into a story about our life.

189
00:07:22,720 --> 00:07:25,400
I'm never able to see things through.

190
00:07:25,400 --> 00:07:27,280
I should have stayed where I was.

191
00:07:27,280 --> 00:07:29,940
When we think like that, it's hard to imagine

192
00:07:29,940 --> 00:07:33,010
what we could still do the next day.

193
00:07:33,060 --> 00:07:35,520
Santiago thinks back to the King of Salem.

194
00:07:35,520 --> 00:07:37,870
Coelho puts forward the idea that when you know

195
00:07:37,870 --> 00:07:40,240
what you want, the world can offer you

196
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:42,880
help in ways you didn't expect.

197
00:07:42,880 --> 00:07:45,400
You may find this idea too optimistic.

198
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:46,460
What speaks to me

199
00:07:46,460 --> 00:07:49,640
is what Santiago manages to do with it.

200
00:07:49,640 --> 00:07:52,320
He starts looking around him again.

201
00:07:52,320 --> 00:07:54,420
At that moment, he's still hungry

202
00:07:54,420 --> 00:07:56,760
and he doesn't know where his money is.

203
00:07:56,760 --> 00:07:59,090
He can still notice something

204
00:07:59,090 --> 00:08:01,280
and offer someone his help.

205
00:08:01,280 --> 00:08:02,330
It doesn't seem like much.

206
00:08:02,380 --> 00:08:04,880
When you think you have no

207
00:08:04,880 --> 00:08:07,280
choice left, it's already a lot.

208
00:08:07,280 --> 00:08:09,430
In front of the merchant's shop,

209
00:08:09,430 --> 00:08:11,510
Santiago sees that the crystal is

210
00:08:11,510 --> 00:08:13,920
dirty and that few people stop.

211
00:08:13,920 --> 00:08:15,410
He offers to clean

212
00:08:15,410 --> 00:08:17,840
the pieces in exchange for a meal.

213
00:08:17,840 --> 00:08:20,840
Two customers come in and buy some crystal.

214
00:08:20,840 --> 00:08:23,760
The merchant ends up giving the boy a job.

215
00:08:23,760 --> 00:08:27,090
I love how simple that first gesture is.

216
00:08:27,140 --> 00:08:29,660
Santiago needs to eat, and the merchant

217
00:08:29,660 --> 00:08:32,440
needs his shop to bring people in.

218
00:08:32,440 --> 00:08:35,010
By noticing the other man's problem, Santiago

219
00:08:35,010 --> 00:08:38,320
finds something he can do for him.

220
00:08:38,320 --> 00:08:40,810
The journey starts again with an offer

221
00:08:40,810 --> 00:08:43,040
he could put into practice that same day.

222
00:08:43,040 --> 00:08:45,680
It's easy to talk about determination

223
00:08:45,680 --> 00:08:48,360
while picturing the moment you succeed.

224
00:08:48,360 --> 00:08:49,340
In the book,

225
00:08:49,340 --> 00:08:52,340
you also have to think of all the days Santiago

226
00:08:52,340 --> 00:08:55,130
goes back to work in the same shop.

227
00:08:55,180 --> 00:08:56,800
He knows the road to

228
00:08:56,800 --> 00:08:58,600
Egypt is still long.

229
00:08:58,600 --> 00:09:00,300
A few days of work

230
00:09:00,300 --> 00:09:02,760
won't be enough to pay for the journey.

231
00:09:02,760 --> 00:09:04,810
He has to stay for months,

232
00:09:04,810 --> 00:09:06,680
learn to serve customers,

233
00:09:06,680 --> 00:09:09,260
try out ideas to sell more

234
00:09:09,260 --> 00:09:11,400
and put money aside.

235
00:09:11,400 --> 00:09:13,080
Putting money aside

236
00:09:13,080 --> 00:09:15,530
means keeping part of what you earn

237
00:09:15,530 --> 00:09:18,000
so you can use it later.

238
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:19,770
You make this gesture many times

239
00:09:19,770 --> 00:09:22,170
before its result can be seen.

240
00:09:22,220 --> 00:09:25,130
Among other things, Santiago suggests serving

241
00:09:25,130 --> 00:09:27,720
tea in crystal glasses.

242
00:09:27,720 --> 00:09:30,540
He noticed that people arrive tired

243
00:09:30,540 --> 00:09:34,000
after the climb, and want something to drink.

244
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:36,550
A detail many passers-by could have

245
00:09:36,550 --> 00:09:39,800
seen becomes an idea because he lingers on it.

246
00:09:39,800 --> 00:09:42,560
He also ends up learning Arabic.

247
00:09:42,560 --> 00:09:44,380
When he arrived, he needed

248
00:09:44,380 --> 00:09:47,020
to find someone who spoke his language.

249
00:09:47,070 --> 00:09:49,490
By the end of his time in the shop,

250
00:09:49,490 --> 00:09:51,380
he has learned to talk with the people

251
00:09:51,380 --> 00:09:53,200
who come in every day.

252
00:09:53,200 --> 00:09:55,250
If you only measure the number

253
00:09:55,250 --> 00:09:57,640
of kilometres he still has to go,

254
00:09:57,640 --> 00:09:59,680
it looks like he hasn't moved forward.

255
00:09:59,680 --> 00:10:02,690
Just think back to his first day

256
00:10:02,690 --> 00:10:05,680
in Tangier to see all that has changed.

257
00:10:05,680 --> 00:10:08,720
When you go through a time like that,

258
00:10:08,720 --> 00:10:11,720
try asking yourself a question simple

259
00:10:11,720 --> 00:10:13,310
enough to have an answer.

260
00:10:13,360 --> 00:10:16,090
What can I do now

261
00:10:16,090 --> 00:10:18,880
that was a problem for me a few months ago?

262
00:10:18,880 --> 00:10:21,030
You may have nothing to show yet

263
00:10:21,030 --> 00:10:23,180
to the people who ask you

264
00:10:23,180 --> 00:10:25,120
when you'll finish your project.

265
00:10:25,120 --> 00:10:26,570
You can still know

266
00:10:26,570 --> 00:10:28,760
that you're getting by a little better.

267
00:10:28,760 --> 00:10:31,080
Se débrouiller means finding

268
00:10:31,080 --> 00:10:33,800
a way to manage with what you have.

269
00:10:33,800 --> 00:10:36,090
You feel your way, you try something,

270
00:10:36,090 --> 00:10:38,810
you ask for a hand if you need one.

271
00:10:38,860 --> 00:10:41,590
These are habits that can help you

272
00:10:41,590 --> 00:10:44,760
long after the time you learned them.

273
00:10:44,760 --> 00:10:47,770
That doesn't make the theft any easier for him.

274
00:10:47,770 --> 00:10:49,920
And it doesn't force anyone to be

275
00:10:49,920 --> 00:10:52,160
happy about a bad experience.

276
00:10:52,160 --> 00:10:53,950
But in this part,

277
00:10:53,950 --> 00:10:56,840
Coelho shows that a problem that seems

278
00:10:56,840 --> 00:10:59,920
to stop a whole life can also make us

279
00:10:59,920 --> 00:11:02,840
look at the next day more closely.

280
00:11:02,840 --> 00:11:05,450
The boy wanted to find a treasure.

281
00:11:05,500 --> 00:11:06,430
After the theft,

282
00:11:06,430 --> 00:11:09,760
he can start by cleaning crystal.

283
00:11:09,760 --> 00:11:12,550
The gap between the two is huge.

284
00:11:12,550 --> 00:11:15,810
And that's exactly why this

285
00:11:15,810 --> 00:11:17,780
scene stays with me when I think

286
00:11:17,780 --> 00:11:20,200
about what we can do after a failure.

287
00:11:20,200 --> 00:11:22,800
There's a third decision that seems

288
00:11:22,800 --> 00:11:26,040
harder to make to me than the first two.

289
00:11:26,040 --> 00:11:29,040
What do you do when you could move on,

290
00:11:29,040 --> 00:11:32,570
but you finally feel good where you are?

291
00:11:32,620 --> 00:11:35,000
After his time with the merchant,

292
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:36,920
Santiago has money.

293
00:11:36,920 --> 00:11:38,680
He knows the work and he has

294
00:11:38,680 --> 00:11:40,960
proved he can make a living from it.

295
00:11:40,960 --> 00:11:43,240
He could stay in business.

296
00:11:43,240 --> 00:11:44,730
He could also go back

297
00:11:44,730 --> 00:11:46,880
to Spain and buy a flock.

298
00:11:46,880 --> 00:11:49,680
Just as he's about to make a choice,

299
00:11:49,680 --> 00:11:52,080
he finds the two stones

300
00:11:52,080 --> 00:11:54,320
the King of Salem gave him.

301
00:11:54,320 --> 00:11:56,200
This small reminder makes him think

302
00:11:56,200 --> 00:11:57,980
of what he was looking for

303
00:11:57,980 --> 00:12:00,450
before the shop became his daily life.

304
00:12:00,450 --> 00:12:03,720
And he chooses to take the road again.

305
00:12:03,720 --> 00:12:05,880
We often talk about leaving behind

306
00:12:05,880 --> 00:12:08,120
a situation that makes us suffer.

307
00:12:08,120 --> 00:12:11,130
This passage is interesting because Santiago

308
00:12:11,130 --> 00:12:14,480
leaves something that did him good.

309
00:12:14,480 --> 00:12:16,440
He is grateful to the merchant.

310
00:12:16,440 --> 00:12:19,550
He also knows that if he stays a little longer,

311
00:12:19,550 --> 00:12:22,680
he'll find new reasons to stay.

312
00:12:22,680 --> 00:12:25,370
At some point, he has to decide what

313
00:12:25,370 --> 00:12:28,590
he wants to give the space he has earned.

314
00:12:28,640 --> 00:12:31,050
The money he has saved can

315
00:12:31,050 --> 00:12:33,360
give him back his old life, or let him

316
00:12:33,360 --> 00:12:35,200
follow an old idea all the way.

317
00:12:35,200 --> 00:12:37,680
He can only spend it once.

318
00:12:37,680 --> 00:12:39,980
The merchant, for his part, has long

319
00:12:39,980 --> 00:12:42,960
thought about his pilgrimage to Mecca.

320
00:12:42,960 --> 00:12:44,850
He keeps this journey in mind because

321
00:12:44,850 --> 00:12:47,560
the idea helps him get through his days.

322
00:12:47,560 --> 00:12:49,640
He's afraid of what he'll feel

323
00:12:49,640 --> 00:12:51,840
once the journey has happened.

324
00:12:51,840 --> 00:12:54,620
This detail touches me because it shows

325
00:12:54,620 --> 00:12:58,170
another reason to keep a dream at a distance.

326
00:12:58,220 --> 00:13:01,200
While we wait, it belongs to us.

327
00:13:01,200 --> 00:13:03,870
We can come back to it on hard days

328
00:13:03,870 --> 00:13:06,120
and imagine it however we like.

329
00:13:06,120 --> 00:13:07,720
The day we try,

330
00:13:07,720 --> 00:13:10,100
we accept that things may take

331
00:13:10,100 --> 00:13:12,240
a shape we hadn't thought of.

332
00:13:12,240 --> 00:13:14,730
We also accept having to find

333
00:13:14,730 --> 00:13:17,760
another reason to get up the next day.

334
00:13:17,760 --> 00:13:21,560
Santiago meets Fatima in an oasis,

335
00:13:21,560 --> 00:13:25,770
a place with water in the middle of the desert.

336
00:13:25,820 --> 00:13:27,800
He loves her and wonders if he

337
00:13:27,800 --> 00:13:30,160
should stay with her.

338
00:13:30,160 --> 00:13:32,810
That's a reason to stop that's very

339
00:13:32,810 --> 00:13:36,000
different from money or work.

340
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:38,340
With Fatima, he can imagine

341
00:13:38,340 --> 00:13:40,400
a future he likes.

342
00:13:40,400 --> 00:13:43,030
Yet she understands why he wants to go

343
00:13:43,030 --> 00:13:45,960
to the pyramids, and urges him to go on.

344
00:13:45,960 --> 00:13:49,120
The alchemist also makes him wonder

345
00:13:49,120 --> 00:13:52,240
what could happen if he stayed

346
00:13:52,240 --> 00:13:54,970
while the urge to leave was still there.

347
00:13:55,020 --> 00:13:56,320
Time would pass.

348
00:13:56,320 --> 00:13:58,510
Santiago might think more and more

349
00:13:58,510 --> 00:14:01,960
about what he never went to see.

350
00:14:01,960 --> 00:14:04,110
And Fatima would also live

351
00:14:04,110 --> 00:14:06,590
with someone still wondering

352
00:14:06,590 --> 00:14:08,920
if he made the right decision.

353
00:14:08,920 --> 00:14:10,970
I love the question this

354
00:14:10,970 --> 00:14:12,640
scene asks the reader.

355
00:14:12,640 --> 00:14:14,790
You can love someone and keep

356
00:14:14,790 --> 00:14:17,320
something you really want to do.

357
00:14:17,320 --> 00:14:19,370
You can be afraid of leaving

358
00:14:19,370 --> 00:14:21,370
and afraid of staying.

359
00:14:21,420 --> 00:14:23,810
When you say these two sentences,

360
00:14:23,810 --> 00:14:25,880
you change one word at the end.

361
00:14:25,880 --> 00:14:27,670
When you feel them,

362
00:14:27,670 --> 00:14:30,960
you can spend months between the two.

363
00:14:30,960 --> 00:14:33,200
Santiago dares to say what he wants

364
00:14:33,200 --> 00:14:36,000
and he listens to what Fatima answers.

365
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:38,520
It makes leaving harder.

366
00:14:38,520 --> 00:14:41,010
It also gives him the right to take

367
00:14:41,010 --> 00:14:45,000
both things that matter to him seriously.

368
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:46,670
The word I keep from this

369
00:14:46,670 --> 00:14:48,730
part is renoncer.

370
00:14:48,780 --> 00:14:52,500
To give something up is to let go

371
00:14:52,500 --> 00:14:55,840
of a possibility you could have kept.

372
00:14:55,840 --> 00:14:58,480
It can be an hour of rest

373
00:14:58,480 --> 00:15:01,320
you value, a way of earning money

374
00:15:01,320 --> 00:15:04,100
that reassures you, or an idea you kept

375
00:15:04,100 --> 00:15:07,400
because thinking of it made you happy.

376
00:15:07,400 --> 00:15:09,480
Santiago can love what he's

377
00:15:09,480 --> 00:15:12,120
leaving and decide to keep going.

378
00:15:12,120 --> 00:15:14,810
That's one reason why this

379
00:15:14,810 --> 00:15:17,280
part feels closer to real life to me

380
00:15:17,280 --> 00:15:19,080
than grand sentences about destiny.

381
00:15:19,080 --> 00:15:21,380
There's a question you can ask yourself

382
00:15:21,380 --> 00:15:23,600
if you feel caught between two paths.

383
00:15:23,600 --> 00:15:25,840
If I stay here one more year,

384
00:15:25,840 --> 00:15:29,640
what will I be glad I said yes to?

385
00:15:29,640 --> 00:15:31,540
And what will I still

386
00:15:31,540 --> 00:15:33,200
want to try?

387
00:15:33,200 --> 00:15:34,840
You have the right to truly

388
00:15:34,840 --> 00:15:37,200
love the life you already have.

389
00:15:37,200 --> 00:15:41,680
In that case, look that answer in the face.

390
00:15:41,680 --> 00:15:44,460
If the other wish comes back, at least tell

391
00:15:44,460 --> 00:15:47,090
yourself what you'd need to make room for.

392
00:15:47,140 --> 00:15:49,530
Often, the idea of leaving everything takes

393
00:15:49,530 --> 00:15:52,400
up so much space that we forget to look at

394
00:15:52,400 --> 00:15:54,000
what we could change first.

395
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:56,480
Santiago leaves the shop.

396
00:15:56,480 --> 00:15:58,530
For you, the choice can start

397
00:15:58,530 --> 00:16:01,120
with something much smaller.

398
00:16:01,120 --> 00:16:02,490
And here we are, back

399
00:16:02,490 --> 00:16:04,600
to the question of motivation.

400
00:16:04,600 --> 00:16:07,720
Enthusiasm can come from a meeting,

401
00:16:07,720 --> 00:16:11,480
an idea, or a word that does us good.

402
00:16:11,480 --> 00:16:13,450
Sometimes it's enough to set off.

403
00:16:13,500 --> 00:16:15,050
What helps Santiago

404
00:16:15,050 --> 00:16:17,840
keep going comes from deeper down.

405
00:16:17,840 --> 00:16:19,440
Several times,

406
00:16:19,440 --> 00:16:22,240
he has the chance to go home or to stay

407
00:16:22,240 --> 00:16:24,800
somewhere he could live happily.

408
00:16:24,800 --> 00:16:26,570
And several times,

409
00:16:26,570 --> 00:16:29,710
when he's alone with the decision, he comes back

410
00:16:29,710 --> 00:16:32,540
to this wish to see the world and discover

411
00:16:32,540 --> 00:16:35,240
what awaits him if he follows his choice through.

412
00:16:35,240 --> 00:16:36,920
No one can tell him

413
00:16:36,920 --> 00:16:39,320
exactly what he'll find.

414
00:16:39,320 --> 00:16:41,690
He knows why he wants to go and see.

415
00:16:41,740 --> 00:16:43,520
In the desert, warriors

416
00:16:43,520 --> 00:16:46,160
take him prisoner with the alchemist.

417
00:16:46,160 --> 00:16:47,960
They give him three days

418
00:16:47,960 --> 00:16:50,920
to do something impossible:

419
00:16:50,920 --> 00:16:53,600
turn himself into the wind.

420
00:16:53,600 --> 00:16:57,260
In this scene, Coelho has the boy speak

421
00:16:57,260 --> 00:17:00,080
with the desert, the wind and the sun.

422
00:17:00,080 --> 00:17:02,520
Santiago passes the test.

423
00:17:02,520 --> 00:17:05,000
We're fully in a fairy tale, and that's

424
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:08,330
exactly what gives this scene its strength.

425
00:17:08,380 --> 00:17:10,330
The boy has to face a fear

426
00:17:10,330 --> 00:17:12,920
he can't solve in advance.

427
00:17:12,920 --> 00:17:15,960
He relies on what he learned by watching

428
00:17:15,960 --> 00:17:18,560
the desert and listening to his guide.

429
00:17:18,560 --> 00:17:21,130
All the attention he paid

430
00:17:21,130 --> 00:17:23,640
to the road helps him just when

431
00:17:23,640 --> 00:17:26,160
he most wants to give in to fear.

432
00:17:26,160 --> 00:17:28,690
When he finally reaches the pyramids,

433
00:17:28,690 --> 00:17:31,600
the book has another surprise for him.

434
00:17:31,600 --> 00:17:34,440
Some men attack him while he's digging.

435
00:17:34,440 --> 00:17:37,530
One of them tells him about a dream he had.

436
00:17:37,580 --> 00:17:39,290
He saw a treasure in Spain,

437
00:17:39,290 --> 00:17:42,200
in an old church, near a tree.

438
00:17:42,200 --> 00:17:44,290
He never wanted to take

439
00:17:44,290 --> 00:17:46,180
the road to look for it.

440
00:17:46,180 --> 00:17:48,680
But Santiago recognises the place.

441
00:17:48,680 --> 00:17:50,990
It's where he used to sleep with his sheep,

442
00:17:50,990 --> 00:17:52,240
before he left.

443
00:17:52,240 --> 00:17:54,050
He goes back to Spain,

444
00:17:54,050 --> 00:17:57,680
digs in the right spot and finds the treasure.

445
00:17:57,680 --> 00:18:00,970
It's a funny way to end an adventure.

446
00:18:01,020 --> 00:18:03,220
Santiago crosses the sea,

447
00:18:03,220 --> 00:18:07,640
spends months working and crosses the desert.

448
00:18:07,640 --> 00:18:11,360
Then he has to go back exactly where he was.

449
00:18:11,360 --> 00:18:12,910
Would he have saved time

450
00:18:12,910 --> 00:18:14,760
by digging right away?

451
00:18:14,760 --> 00:18:18,240
But who would he have asked where to dig?

452
00:18:18,240 --> 00:18:20,830
The dream that shows him the right spot

453
00:18:20,830 --> 00:18:23,600
is told to him near the pyramids.

454
00:18:23,600 --> 00:18:26,440
Someone far from home had to

455
00:18:26,440 --> 00:18:29,970
tell him about a place he already knew.

456
00:18:30,020 --> 00:18:31,740
I think this ending says a lot

457
00:18:31,740 --> 00:18:34,120
about the things we discover by trying.

458
00:18:34,120 --> 00:18:35,200
Before setting off,

459
00:18:35,200 --> 00:18:37,810
we don't always know what information

460
00:18:37,810 --> 00:18:40,520
we're missing, or who can give it to us.

461
00:18:40,520 --> 00:18:43,160
And what if Santiago had come home without gold?

462
00:18:43,160 --> 00:18:46,480
Really ask yourself that for a second.

463
00:18:46,480 --> 00:18:48,880
He would still have met Fatima.

464
00:18:48,880 --> 00:18:50,700
He would have learned to earn a living

465
00:18:50,700 --> 00:18:52,660
in Tangier and to speak a language

466
00:18:52,660 --> 00:18:55,010
he didn't understand at first.

467
00:18:55,060 --> 00:18:57,750
He'd have thought of his father, the King of Salem

468
00:18:57,750 --> 00:19:00,600
and the merchant he worked with.

469
00:19:00,600 --> 00:19:03,190
He'd also have known he could make

470
00:19:03,190 --> 00:19:05,880
a new decision after a failure.

471
00:19:05,880 --> 00:19:07,770
That's a lot for someone

472
00:19:07,770 --> 00:19:10,100
who woke up one day with a dream

473
00:19:10,100 --> 00:19:12,800
and didn't know what to do with it.

474
00:19:12,800 --> 00:19:14,880
The gold makes the ending happy.

475
00:19:14,880 --> 00:19:17,220
The people he met and the trials

476
00:19:17,220 --> 00:19:19,600
give the return its value.

477
00:19:19,600 --> 00:19:21,330
What touches me in this book

478
00:19:21,330 --> 00:19:23,260
is that the reason to keep going

479
00:19:23,260 --> 00:19:24,890
can stay very simple.

480
00:19:24,940 --> 00:19:27,960
I want to know what I'll discover.

481
00:19:27,960 --> 00:19:29,640
I want to see what I can

482
00:19:29,640 --> 00:19:31,920
do with what I've learned.

483
00:19:31,920 --> 00:19:33,560
I want to go far enough

484
00:19:33,560 --> 00:19:36,360
to be able to answer for myself.

485
00:19:36,360 --> 00:19:39,140
As long as one of these sentences still

486
00:19:39,140 --> 00:19:41,720
means something to you, it can help you

487
00:19:41,720 --> 00:19:44,640
get through days when you have less energy.

488
00:19:44,640 --> 00:19:47,640
And if you find another reason along the way,

489
00:19:47,640 --> 00:19:49,840
take it seriously too.

490
00:19:49,840 --> 00:19:52,540
Santiago sets off to look for one thing.

491
00:19:52,590 --> 00:19:54,420
In the end, he has several reasons

492
00:19:54,420 --> 00:19:56,400
to be glad he left.

493
00:19:56,400 --> 00:19:58,300
I'd recommend The Alchemist

494
00:19:58,300 --> 00:20:00,070
to someone who has been asking

495
00:20:00,070 --> 00:20:02,800
the same question about their life for years.

496
00:20:02,800 --> 00:20:04,900
It's a tale that's easy to follow,

497
00:20:04,900 --> 00:20:07,680
and sometimes very sure of its answers.

498
00:20:07,680 --> 00:20:09,550
For my part, I mostly love

499
00:20:09,550 --> 00:20:12,440
the moments when the characters hesitate.

500
00:20:12,440 --> 00:20:16,080
The father opens his hand to give his gold coins.

501
00:20:16,080 --> 00:20:19,250
The merchant talks about a trip he loves to await.

502
00:20:19,300 --> 00:20:21,450
Fatima listens to someone she

503
00:20:21,450 --> 00:20:23,720
would like to keep close to her.

504
00:20:23,720 --> 00:20:26,210
In those moments, we get to know a little better

505
00:20:26,210 --> 00:20:28,400
the people who choose, and we can

506
00:20:28,400 --> 00:20:31,120
wonder what we would have done in their place.

507
00:20:31,120 --> 00:20:33,170
These are pages you can

508
00:20:33,170 --> 00:20:35,840
come back to when your own answer has changed.

509
00:20:35,840 --> 00:20:39,500
If you have a dream you've kept for a long time,

510
00:20:39,500 --> 00:20:42,320
give yourself a minute to name it.

511
00:20:42,320 --> 00:20:44,960
You can start with the simplest words.

512
00:20:45,010 --> 00:20:47,700
I've always wanted to…

513
00:20:47,700 --> 00:20:50,430
Then ask yourself what first

514
00:20:50,430 --> 00:20:53,080
step would make sense today.

515
00:20:53,080 --> 00:20:55,710
The answer might fit into an hour,

516
00:20:55,710 --> 00:20:58,000
a date, or a name to look up.

517
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:00,170
It may also remind you why

518
00:21:00,170 --> 00:21:02,880
you wanted to try the first time.

519
00:21:02,880 --> 00:21:05,200
And if you've been waiting for years,

520
00:21:05,200 --> 00:21:08,080
you still have the right to ask the question

521
00:21:08,080 --> 00:21:10,120
with the person you are today.

522
00:21:10,120 --> 00:21:13,570
Thank you for listening to Cozy French Books.

523
00:21:13,620 --> 00:21:16,630
If this reading made you think of a dream

524
00:21:16,630 --> 00:21:19,150
or of a choice you had to make,

525
00:21:19,150 --> 00:21:20,560
tell me about it in the comments.

526
00:21:20,560 --> 00:21:23,560
You can write a single sentence in French.

527
00:21:23,560 --> 00:21:26,640
I've always wanted to…, or maybe,

528
00:21:26,640 --> 00:21:29,960
one day, I decided to take the plunge.

529
00:21:29,960 --> 00:21:32,270
If this episode spoke to you,

530
00:21:32,270 --> 00:21:34,440
you can share it with someone

531
00:21:34,440 --> 00:21:36,820
the book might touch, and subscribe

532
00:21:36,820 --> 00:21:38,770
so we can open another one together.

533
00:21:38,820 --> 00:21:40,770
I'm Léa.

534
00:21:40,770 --> 00:21:43,640
Take care of yourself, and see you very soon.
